<Page
	title="About"
	description="Harry Munroe works at IBM and is a fellow at the National Defense University's Eisenhower School, studying how allies build and adopt new technology."
>
	<div class="prose">
		<h2>What I do</h2>
		<p>
			I study how a new technology becomes something a country can rely on. Inventing it is the
			first step. Someone then has to build it at scale, institutions have to adopt it, and
			someone has to pay for the capacity, often before the orders exist. I'm most interested in
			how allies do this together, the United States and the Nordic countries in particular.
		</p>
		<p>
			I have spent nearly ten years at IBM. Most recently I led engagements at IBM's Innovation
			Studio in Washington, DC, where I helped federal agencies and their contractors work out
			what AI, quantum computing, and other emerging technologies could do for them. Before that
			I worked in healthcare IT consulting and in technology sales and enablement. Across all of
			it, my job has been to take what is being invented and explain it to the people who have to
			decide what to do with it.
		</p>
		<p>
			My background is in operations research and finance. So I tend to ask practical questions:
			what new capacity costs, who carries the risk, how long it takes to build, and what has to
			be true before a supplier will commit.
		</p>
		<p>
			This year I'm a private-sector fellow at the Dwight D. Eisenhower School for National
			Security and Resource Strategy at the National Defense University, sponsored by IBM. I'm
			studying defense acquisition, finance, and how allies organize industrial production.
		</p>

		<h2>Background</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>IBM Consulting, Washington, DC. IBM Quantum Ambassador and Qiskit Advocate.</li>
			<li>Eisenhower School, National Defense University, 2026 to 2027.</li>
			<li>Columbia University, undergraduate studies in operations research and finance.</li>
			<li>Master of Public Health. PMP and CPHIMS certifications.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Who I write for</h2>
		<p>
			The essays and explainers here are for people with real responsibility who privately
			suspect they're still beginners. The director whose domain expertise got her the seat, but
			who now fields questions about AI she can't confidently answer. The official who signs off
			on infrastructure he won't live to see finished. If you've ever sat in a meeting nodding at
			a term you couldn't define, this site is for you.
		</p>

		<h2>Where I'm from</h2>
		<p>
			I'm from New England, and I have a long interest in the Nordic countries. They are both
			northern, forested, maritime places working on the same questions about energy,
			resilience, and how to keep sparsely settled communities connected over long distances.
		</p>
		<p>
			I collect ideas the way some people collect old issues of National Geographic. This site is
			where I put them to work.
		</p>

